    {
      "id": "OiqOmq7PuUKrQQA_kqc_a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 872.96875,
      "y": 955.3125,
      "width": 497.4195556640625,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aT",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 290016626,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 375789810,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787511580524,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Handling Failures\nFailure detection: gossip protocol\nTemporary failures: sloppy quorum + hinted handoff\nPermanent failures: anti-entropy + Merkle trees",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Handling Failures\nFailure detection: gossip protocol\nTemporary failures: sloppy quorum + hinted handoff\nPermanent failures: anti-entropy + Merkle trees",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1MGC8aOIJoNAszeAd21QH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1576.875,
      "y": 809.875,
      "width": 1087.37890625,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aU",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1727863086,
      "version": 16,
      "versionNonce": 1587870962,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787512223379,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Gossip protocol (failure detection)\nEvery node keeps a membership list with a heartbeat counter for each peer.\nOnce every second or so, a node picks 1-3 random peers and exchanges its membership list with them, \nmerging in whichever heartbeat counts are newer. Because each round spreads info to new random peers, \nknowledge of \"who's alive\" propagates exponentially — like a rumor — until the whole cluster converges \nwithout any central coordinator. If a node's heartbeat hasn't increased for a threshold time, it's marked down.\n\nSloppy quorum (temporary failures)\nNormal quorum reads/writes require exactly the N nodes that \"own\" a key on the hash ring.\nSloppy quorum relaxes this: if one of those N nodes is down, the request just uses the next healthy node on \nthe ring instead — so you always get W or R responses to satisfy the quorum, even during a partition. \nThis trades strict correctness for availability: you might read slightly stale data, but writes never block\njust because one designated node is unreachable.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Gossip protocol (failure detection)\nEvery node keeps a membership list with a heartbeat counter for each peer.\nOnce every second or so, a node picks 1-3 random peers and exchanges its membership list with them, \nmerging in whichever heartbeat counts are newer. Because each round spreads info to new random peers, \nknowledge of \"who's alive\" propagates exponentially — like a rumor — until the whole cluster converges \nwithout any central coordinator. If a node's heartbeat hasn't increased for a threshold time, it's marked down.\n\nSloppy quorum (temporary failures)\nNormal quorum reads/writes require exactly the N nodes that \"own\" a key on the hash ring.\nSloppy quorum relaxes this: if one of those N nodes is down, the request just uses the next healthy node on \nthe ring instead — so you always get W or R responses to satisfy the quorum, even during a partition. \nThis trades strict correctness for availability: you might read slightly stale data, but writes never block\njust because one designated node is unreachable.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "gGcCuqk4hlzLTbWlUO_SL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1584.375,
      "y": 1181.90625,
      "width": 1012.0989990234375,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 960378354,
      "version": 19,
      "versionNonce": 1115134642,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787512138627,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Hinted handoff (temporary failures)\nThis is the mechanism that makes sloppy quorum work correctly. When a write lands on a \nsubstitute node (because the real owner is down), that substitute stores the data along with a \n\"hint\" metadata tag saying which node it actually belongs to. It's a temporary parking spot, not a \npermanent replica. Once the original node recovers, the substitute node detects this (via gossip) and\nforwards the hinted data back, then deletes its own copy. Net effect: no write is lost, and data \neventually lands back where it belongs.\n\nAnti-entropy + Merkle trees (permanent failures)\nWhen a node is down long enough that hinted handoff isn't enough (or data has diverged in general),\nreplicas run anti-entropy — a background sync process that compares two replicas' data and reconciles \ndifferences. Comparing every key directly is too slow, so each replica builds a Merkle tree: leaves are \nhashes of small key ranges, and each parent is a hash of its children, all the way up to one root hash.\nTwo replicas start by comparing root hashes — if equal, they're identical, done. If not, they recurse \ndown only the branches whose hashes differ, quickly isolating just the handful of keys that actually \nchanged instead of scanning the whole dataset.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Hinted handoff (temporary failures)\nThis is the mechanism that makes sloppy quorum work correctly. When a write lands on a \nsubstitute node (because the real owner is down), that substitute stores the data along with a \n\"hint\" metadata tag saying which node it actually belongs to. It's a temporary parking spot, not a \npermanent replica. Once the original node recovers, the substitute node detects this (via gossip) and\nforwards the hinted data back, then deletes its own copy. Net effect: no write is lost, and data \neventually lands back where it belongs.\n\nAnti-entropy + Merkle trees (permanent failures)\nWhen a node is down long enough that hinted handoff isn't enough (or data has diverged in general),\nreplicas run anti-entropy — a background sync process that compares two replicas' data and reconciles \ndifferences. Comparing every key directly is too slow, so each replica builds a Merkle tree: leaves are \nhashes of small key ranges, and each parent is a hash of its children, all the way up to one root hash.\nTwo replicas start by comparing root hashes — if equal, they're identical, done. If not, they recurse \ndown only the branches whose hashes differ, quickly isolating just the handful of keys that actually \nchanged instead of scanning the whole dataset.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
